{
  "summary": "The proposal has several significant gaps: (1) It conflates 'existing tmux session' with 'running TUI' — a TUI started directly in a terminal has no tmux session to attach to, so the check-and-attach approach only works when the user set up the systemd service. The lock file (not tmux) is the authoritative indicator of a running TUI. (2) Using `os.execvp()` is fragile — if the tmux session dies mid-attach or is in a broken state (e.g., from a crashed service), the user gets dumped with no error context; a subprocess call with proper error handling and fallback messaging would be more robust. (3) The `--new` flag is misleading since the lock mechanism already prevents a second TUI instance — the flag can never actually start a fresh TUI while one is running, making it dead code. (4) The proposal ignores nested tmux: if the user is already inside a tmux session, `tmux attach` creates a confusing nested-tmux situation that should either be detected and warned about, or handled via `tmux switch-client`. (5) The config options (`tmux.auto_attach`, `tmux.session_name`) add unnecessary surface area — the session name is already deterministic from `tmux_session_name()` and auto-attach should simply be the default behavior when a service-managed session exists, not a toggleable preference. A better approach: check the lock file first; if locked, then check `tmux has-session`; if a session exists, attach (or switch-client if already in tmux); if no session exists, print a message saying the TUI is running elsewhere and how to find it."
}
